sortnetopt-prefixcert-v3 — prefix-rooted certificates: gen-proof learns a root

Applies on top of the ten-patch v3 stack, in this order:

    sortnetopt-macos-proc.patch
    sortnetopt-instrumentation-v3.patch
    sortnetopt-online-subsumption-v3.patch
    sortnetopt-perf-v3.patch
    sortnetopt-tier1-v3.patch
    sortnetopt-tier2a-v3.patch
    sortnetopt-tier2b-v3.patch
    sortnetopt-limits-v3.patch
    sortnetopt-zm-v3.patch
    sortnetopt-decomp-v3.patch
    sortnetopt-prefixcert-v3.patch  <- this one

Base pin: 0b5d09c47446096f9e3a0812b35afc72b7f2a718.
Nothing in checker/ is touched. The frozen snocheck is never rebuilt, and the
certificates it reads are byte-identical to what the ten-patch stack emitted.

Two files change: src/proof.rs and src/bin/sortnetopt.rs. No search heuristic,
no pruning rule, no bound, no new proof rule.


================================================================================
THE BLOCKER
================================================================================

`gen_proof` ended with

    gen.encode_proof(&Arc::new(OutputSet::all_values(max_channels)));

— the root of every certificate was hard-coded to the full cube. A decomposed
job (docs/lowmem-endgame-assessment.md §11, tools/class_campaign.py) searches
from the output set of a comparator prefix P, so the full cube is not in that
job's pruned index at all, and `encode_proof`'s

    let bound = self.output_sets.get(target).unwrap().0;   // proof.rs:237

panicked. Per-job certificates are a contract requirement — a class result with
no independently checkable certificate per job is not load-bearing — so this
was the last hard blocker before real class jobs (evidence/v3/decomp/report.md,
"Blockers before a first real class job", item 1).


================================================================================
WHAT THE PATCH DOES
================================================================================

    sortnetopt gen-proof <input> [-p a b [-p c d ...]] [--prefix-root]

`-p` takes flat comparator pairs, exactly as `search -p` does, and must be the
same prefix the `search` that produced `<input>` was run with. `--prefix-root`
forces the prefix container even for an empty prefix (a depth-0 job is the full
problem, but a campaign that composes prefix claims must not silently receive a
full-problem certificate for one of its jobs).

With no prefix the path is **unchanged**: same root, same container choice,
same bytes. Verified, not asserted — see EVIDENCE.

With a prefix the certificate is emitted in the v2p container, specified in
docs/certificate-format-v2.md §9 (a new section; §§1-8 are untouched). The
claim it makes is NOT `S(n) >= b`. It is

    let X_P be the prefix applied to the full n-cube; then s(X_P) >= b,

i.e. any n-channel sorting network beginning with exactly P has at least
L + b comparators.


================================================================================
THE ROOT IS A WITNESS, NOT A NEW RULE
================================================================================

The engine memoises a prefix job's root under `canonicalize(true)`, and
`prune-all` may further replace that canonical set by any set subsuming it. So
the root of the proof DAG is in general NOT `X_P` but some `W` with

    permute(perm, invert?(W))  ⊆  X_P

— which is exactly the shape of the `getBound` witness the checker already
applies to every interior step (Check.hs / Checker.thy:425-445). Rather than
reconstruct the canonicalisation transform (whose convention would then have to
be re-derived and re-trusted), `encode_prefix_root` resolves the root through
`lookup_witness`: the *same* call that produces every other witness in the
file. It returns the largest bound over all subsuming sets together with the
transform, already in the certificate's perm convention.

The prefix section therefore stores `(invert, perm, step_id)` and the checker
re-uses its existing `getBound` on it verbatim. No new proof rule, no new
witness kind, no change to any step payload byte.

Soundness (docs/certificate-format-v2.md §9.4 P5): a network sorting X_P sorts
any subset of it, and channel permutation and complementation carry sorting
networks to sorting networks, so s(X_P) >= s(W) >= bound(W) >= b.

The stored packed `X_P` is a *mirror*: a checker MUST recompute it by applying
P to the full cube and reject on mismatch (§9.4 P3). That check is what binds
the certificate to its prefix, and it is what makes a certificate-for-the-wrong-
prefix a rejection rather than a silently valid proof of the wrong proposition.


================================================================================
WHY A THIRD CONTAINER RATHER THAN A FLAG ON v2
================================================================================

v2p is `format_version = 3` with `flags` bit 0 set and `table_offset` moved
past the prefix section. The v2 spec (§3.2) already requires a reader to reject
`format_version != 2`, to reject non-zero `flags`, and to reject
`table_offset != 64`. A v2p file therefore trips three independent rejections
in any reader written to the v2 spec, before it can misread anything.

That is the point. A prefix certificate proves a different proposition from a
full-problem certificate, and the failure mode to design against is a tool
reporting a prefix bound as if it were S(n). `do_check` in tools/cert_v2.py
refuses v2p input for the same reason; only `prefix-check`/`do_check_prefix`
will read one, and they report the claim as a prefix claim.

The frozen verified checker cannot read v2p and is not asked to. The
full-problem v1 path — the only path with a verified checker behind it — is
untouched, and the n=9/n=10 pipelines continue to run through `snocheck -v` as
the anchor. That per-job certificates are checked today by unverified code is a
recorded gap, not a hidden one (§9.8).


================================================================================
COMPOSITION IS NOT IN THE CERTIFICATE
================================================================================

A single certificate says nothing about S(n). The class answer is

    S(n)  >=  min over P in 𝒫 of ( L + b_P )

and it needs three things no certificate can discharge: 𝒫 exhaustive at depth
L, each certificate's (n, P) equal to its job's, and every job covered.
tools/class_campaign.py `verify` checks all three — it rebuilds the depth-L
frontier from the manifest's own parameters, re-runs tools/cert_v2.py
prefix-check on every job's certificate file against the job's prefix, detects
sha256 drift of the certificate on disk, and only then prints COMPOSITION
VERIFIED. See docs/certificate-format-v2.md §9.6.

One asymmetry is deliberate: a certified bound *below* the search's
lower_bound is a WARNING and the composition then uses the certified bound
(sound, just weaker — a pruned root replaced by a weaker subsumer). A certified
bound *above* it is a hard failure, because that is a contradiction.


================================================================================
EVIDENCE
================================================================================

Legacy path byte-identical, measured on a FIXED pruned directory (proof.bin is
not reproducible across *searches* — nondeterministic search order — so the
emitter must be tested on fixed input):

    n=8  72030bf39eaf2d8b2b382af65b5a7fb15748da88965671435874215d9b1deb9f
    n=9  337c649b03b54f96b83caaafefde4cf330d8150cc91d00831a812b8c00d397a6

Each is the sha256 of proof.bin from BOTH the ten-patch binary
(cb7b4b6d56a0a8ed6da5c30b252d248a7b5e7ab7c868842486f1602660beb199) and the
eleven-patch binary
(15352938e26af571968146aeadf2e4764658abc4e139e6be4c1f69586ddcb556) run on the
same pruned directory; `cmp` reports no difference.

Frozen verified checker, unchanged, on certificates from the patched binary:
`Just (9,25)` and `Just (10,29)` — on the default build and on the wide12 and
wide13 builds. 35/35 release tests on all three builds.

Two independent implementations of §9 agree on real engine output:
tools/cert_v2.py accepts every certificate this emitter produces and rejects
every one of nine corruptions of it (section magic, stored root set, section
digest, format_version, flags, truncated trailer, raised claimed_bound, altered
prefix comparator, duplicated root perm entry, out-of-range witness step) —
25/25 selftest lines on an n=8 L=1 certificate.

End-to-end campaigns at n=9 and n=10, depths 1 and 2, every job certified and
every certificate re-verified: composed answers 25, 25, 29, 29 — exactly S(9)
and S(10), COMPOSITION VERIFIED in all four.

wide13 n=13 --limit 42 reproduces: result 42, 50,285,534 states (-1.2% of the
reference 50.9M; search order is nondeterministic), peak memory footprint
3.34 GB against a 3.35 GB reference, inside the 5 GB guardrail.


================================================================================
NOT DONE, ON PURPOSE
================================================================================

* `fix.rs` (`fix-proof`) is not prefix-aware. It was already not v2-aware and
  is not in the pipeline; noted rather than half-fixed.
* No verified-checker support for v2p. Separate campaign, §9.8.
* Handing a v2 or v2p file to the frozen checker makes it read the ASCII magic
  as a u32 step count of 1,129,270,867 and attempt a ~13 GB table read. This
  hazard predates this patch (it applies to v2 already); it is recorded here
  because prefix jobs make v2p files common, and a wrapper script that guards
  on the magic before invoking snocheck would be cheap insurance.

Nowhere in this patch is any comparator count used as a search target, bound,
feature or stopping condition.

